	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q53",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to Beck v. Ohio (1964), as cited in the IRG, how is probable cause judged?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "By the officer's subjective good faith alone" },
		{ id: "B", text: "By whether a reasonable person would believe a suspect has committed, is committing, or is about to commit a crime" },
		{ id: "C", text: "By the officer's training and experience alone" },
		{ id: "D", text: "By a preponderance of the evidence" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Beck v. Ohio holds that probable cause is an objective standard: whether the facts would warrant a reasonable person to believe a suspect has committed, is committing, or is about to commit a crime. Subjective good faith alone is not the test. IRG Ch. 10, §10.1. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q54",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "In Brower v. Inyo County (1989), the 'dead man's roadblock' case, when does a seizure occur under the Fourth Amendment?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Only when the suspect is physically handcuffed by an officer" },
		{ id: "B", text: "When police intentionally use a roadblock to stop a suspect" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Only when the roadblock is set up on a public highway" },
		{ id: "D", text: "A roadblock is never a seizure under the Fourth Amendment" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Brower v. Inyo County held that a seizure occurs under the Fourth Amendment when police intentionally use a roadblock to stop a suspect. IRG Ch. 34, §34.4; Federal Cases list. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q56",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "An officer approaches a traveler in an airport terminal, identifies himself, and asks the traveler questions about his travel. Under Florida v. Royer (1983), what may the traveler do?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Refuse to identify himself, refuse to cooperate, refuse to answer questions, and simply walk away" },
		{ id: "B", text: "He must answer the officer's questions until the officer releases him" },
		{ id: "C", text: "His refusal to answer, by itself, gives the officer probable cause to arrest him" },
		{ id: "D", text: "He must consent to a search of his luggage before he is allowed to leave" }
	  ],
	  answer: "A",
	  explain: "Florida v. Royer stands for the principle that a person approached by officers in a public place may refuse to identify himself, refuse to cooperate, refuse to answer questions, and simply walk away. IRG Ch. 10, §10.2. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q57",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to Kentucky v. King (2011), officers knock on an apartment door, announce themselves, and then hear noises suggesting evidence is being destroyed. What does the case hold about how the exigency arose?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "The knock itself created the exigency, making the warrantless entry unlawful" },
		{ id: "B", text: "Officers may create an exigency whenever they have probable cause" },
		{ id: "C", text: "The officers did not create the exigency; the occupants' reaction did, and officers may not create an exigency by engaging in or threatening conduct that violates the Fourth Amendment" },
		{ id: "D", text: "Any noise inside a home automatically creates exigent circumstances" }
	  ],
	  answer: "C",
	  explain: "Kentucky v. King holds that peace officers cannot create the exigency by engaging or threatening to engage in conduct that violates the Fourth Amendment. In this case the officers did not create the circumstances; the reaction of the occupants did. IRG Ch. 10, §10.8. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q58",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to Maryland v. Shatzer (2010), after a person in custody requests an attorney and interrogation must cease, when may officers resume questioning without an attorney present?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "After 24 hours have passed" },
		{ id: "B", text: "After at least a fourteen-day break in Miranda custody" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Immediately, if a different officer asks the questions" },
		{ id: "D", text: "As soon as the person is released on bond" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Maryland v. Shatzer holds that once a person in custody requests an attorney, interrogations must cease until an attorney is present or, alternatively, until there is at least a fourteen-day break in Miranda custody. IRG Ch. 10, §10.6. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q60",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to the IRG instructor note on Missouri v. McNeely (2013), when may an officer use Transportation Code §724.012(b) to secure a mandatory blood specimen from a DWI suspect?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Whenever the driver refuses a breath test" },
		{ id: "B", text: "In every DWI arrest involving a crash" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Only in exigent circumstances; otherwise a blood search warrant should be used to secure a non-voluntary specimen" },
		{ id: "D", text: "Whenever the driver has a prior DWI conviction" }
	  ],
	  answer: "C",
	  explain: "Per the IRG instructor note on Missouri v. McNeely, Transportation Code §724.012(b) should not be used to secure a mandatory specimen except in exigent circumstances. A blood search warrant should be used to secure a non-voluntary specimen. IRG Ch. 22, §22.29. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q61",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "Under Code of Criminal Procedure Art. 14.051 and United States v. Santana (1976), a peace officer from another state who is in fresh pursuit of a person reasonably suspected of a felony may do what?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Continue the pursuit into Texas and arrest the person" },
		{ id: "B", text: "Enter Texas only to observe, and must wait for a Texas officer to make the arrest" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Continue the pursuit into Texas only for misdemeanor offenses" },
		{ id: "D", text: "Arrest the person only after first obtaining a Texas warrant" }
	  ],
	  answer: "A",
	  explain: "Under CCP Art. 14.051, fresh pursuit means a pursuit without unreasonable delay of a person an officer reasonably suspects has committed a felony, and an out-of-state officer in fresh pursuit may continue the pursuit into Texas and arrest the person. United States v. Santana is the court case cited for this rule. IRG Ch. 10, §10.8. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q62",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to United States v. Sokolow (1989), reasonable suspicion is based on what standard?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "A single suspicious act, standing alone" },
		{ id: "B", text: "The officer's subjective good faith" },
		{ id: "C", text: "The totality of the circumstances, and it is a lesser burden than probable cause" },
		{ id: "D", text: "A preponderance of the evidence" }
	  ],
	  answer: "C",
	  explain: "United States v. Sokolow holds that reasonable suspicion is based upon the totality of the circumstances and is a lesser burden than probable cause. IRG Ch. 10, §10.1. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q63",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "According to the case cited in the IRG as Brigham City, Utah v. Smart (2006), what permits a warrantless entry into a dwelling?",
