	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q67",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "In Curtis v. State, what did the Texas Court of Criminal Appeals hold about a traffic stop based on a vehicle weaving?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Weaving alone can never support reasonable suspicion for a stop" },
		{ id: "B", text: "The stop for failure to maintain a single marked lane was reasonable, and the officer's training and experience, most specifically in DWI enforcement, was taken into account" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Only a marked-lane violation observed by two officers can support a stop" },
		{ id: "D", text: "The stop was unreasonable because weaving is not a traffic offense" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Curtis v. State held that reasonable suspicion supported the stop based on a vehicle weaving. The training and experience of the officer, most specifically as it applies to DWI enforcement, was taken into account, and the stop for failure to maintain a single marked lane was reasonable. IRG Ch. 23, §23.3. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
