		{ id: "B", text: "An excessive force claim, because the officers were placed on notice of the suspect's complaints" },
		{ id: "C", text: "A Miranda violation, because the suspect was not warned before being handcuffed" },
		{ id: "D", text: "A false arrest claim, because the handcuffs were applied before the arrest was complete" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Per IRG Ch. 31, §31.12, Baskin v. Smith, 50 Fed Appx. 731 (2002): handcuffing too tightly and failing to double-lock the handcuffs may lead to an excessive force claim when the officers have been placed on notice of a suspect's complaints, especially if the suspect expresses a medical condition aggravated by the handcuffs. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
