	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q100",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "A suspect sees officers approaching and runs. As he flees, he tosses a bag of drugs to the ground. Officers tackle him and recover the drugs. Under California v. Hodari D. (1991), was the suspect seized before he dropped the drugs?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Yes, he was seized the moment officers began pursuing him" },
		{ id: "B", text: "No, a seizure requires physical force or submission to authority, and he had not submitted" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Yes, because the officers' show of authority alone is a seizure" },
		{ id: "D", text: "No, because a seizure requires a warrant" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "California v. Hodari D. holds that a seizure requires either physical force or submission to authority. A suspect who flees has not submitted, so he was not seized before dropping the drugs, and the abandoned drugs are not the fruit of a seizure. Listed in IRG Ch. 35, §35.8 (pedestrian stops) with Terry v. Ohio; holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q101",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "Officers in a patrol car drive alongside a man walking down the street, watching him. The man keeps walking and does not stop. Under Michigan v. Chesternut (1988), has a seizure occurred?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Yes, any time officers follow a person it is a seizure" },
		{ id: "B", text: "No, police following a person is not a seizure if a reasonable person would feel free to leave or terminate the encounter" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Yes, because the patrol car is a show of authority" },
		{ id: "D", text: "No, because officers may never seize a pedestrian without a warrant" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Michigan v. Chesternut holds that police conduct such as driving alongside a pedestrian is not a seizure unless it is so intimidating that a reasonable person would not feel free to leave or terminate the encounter. Listed in IRG Ch. 35, §35.8 (pedestrian stops) with Terry v. Ohio; holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q102",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "A Virginia officer arrests a driver for driving on a suspended license, a misdemeanor, even though Virginia law says the officer should issue a summons instead. Under Virginia v. Moore (2008), is the arrest lawful under the Fourth Amendment?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "No, because the officer violated state law, the arrest violates the Fourth Amendment" },
		{ id: "B", text: "Yes, the arrest does not violate the Fourth Amendment, and a search incident to the arrest is lawful" },
		{ id: "C", text: "No, because a warrantless arrest is never allowed for a misdemeanor" },
		{ id: "D", text: "Yes, but only if the officer first obtains a warrant" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Virginia v. Moore holds that a warrantless arrest for a minor offense does not violate the Fourth Amendment even when state law directs the officer to issue a summons instead, and a search incident to that arrest is lawful. Listed in IRG Ch. 6 (Racial Profiling) among the traffic-stop cases; holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q103",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "Before taking a written statement, an officer warns a suspect that his statement may be used \"for or against him\" in court. Under Creager v. State (1997) and Code of Criminal Procedure Art. 38.22, §2(a)(1), what is the result?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "The statement is admissible, because the warning covers both possibilities" },
		{ id: "B", text: "The statement is inadmissible per se, because the warning must say the statement may be used against him, not for or against him" },
		{ id: "C", text: "The statement is admissible if the suspect signed a waiver anyway" },
		{ id: "D", text: "The statement is admissible, because only Miranda warnings matter, not Art. 38.22" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Creager v. State holds that warning a suspect his statement may be used \"for or against him\" misstates the Art. 38.22, §2(a)(1) warning, which must say the statement may be used against him in court; a statement taken after that misstatement is inadmissible per se. (Improper remarks made during interrogation after proper warnings are only a factor in voluntariness, not per se inadmissible.) Cited in IRG Ch. 10, §10.6 under CCP Art. 38.22; holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q104",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "A sixteen-year-old is brought before a magistrate, who gives the required warnings and asks officers to return the juvenile and the recording of his statement afterward so the magistrate can decide whether the statement was voluntary, under Family Code §51.095(f). Officers never bring him back, and the magistrate makes no voluntariness determination. Under State v. Torres (2023), are the juvenile's statements admissible?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Yes, because the magistrate never completed the procedure" },
		{ id: "B", text: "No, because the magistrate used the §51.095(f) procedure and no voluntariness determination was made, the statements are inadmissible" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Yes, because the juvenile was sixteen and old enough to waive his rights alone" },
		{ id: "D", text: "No, but only if the juvenile asked for an attorney" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "State v. Torres holds that when a magistrate invokes the Family Code §51.095(f) procedure by asking officers to return the juvenile and the recording for a voluntariness determination, and officers fail to do so, the magistrate still \"used\" the procedure and the statements are inadmissible because no voluntariness determination was made. IRG Ch. 33 (Juvenile Offenders) includes the case caption and the Court of Criminal Appeals affirmance; holding is from the case itself. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q105",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "Three men agree to commit a single robbery together and are caught after that one crime. Under Nguyen v. State (1999), does that agreement alone prove a \"combination\" for engaging in organized criminal activity under Penal Code §71.02?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Yes, any agreement among three or more people to commit a crime is a combination" },
		{ id: "B", text: "No, the State must prove more than an agreement to commit a single crime; it must show intent to participate in a group carrying on a continuing course of criminal activities" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Yes, but only if the crime is a felony" },
		{ id: "D", text: "No, because a combination requires at least five people" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Nguyen v. State holds that \"collaborate in carrying on criminal activities\" (Penal Code §71.01(a)) cannot mean merely an agreement to jointly commit a single crime; the State must prove the members intended to work together in a continuing course of criminal activities. Cited in IRG Ch. 8, §8.35 (organized crime); holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	},
	{
	  id: "TCOLE_case_q106",
	  subject: "Case Law",
	  points: 1,
	  prompt: "A deputy sheriff is charged with a civil rights violation causing a prisoner's death, but the jury convicts him only of a lesser assault and acquits him of causing the death. As a condition of probation, the judge orders him to pay the prisoner's funeral expenses. Under Gordon v. State (1986), is that restitution order lawful?",
	  options: [
		{ id: "A", text: "Yes, restitution may cover any loss caused by the defendant's conduct" },
		{ id: "B", text: "No, restitution may only cover losses from an offense the defendant was found criminally responsible for, and he was acquitted of causing the death" },
		{ id: "C", text: "Yes, because funeral expenses are always recoverable" },
		{ id: "D", text: "No, because restitution is never allowed as a condition of probation" }
	  ],
	  answer: "B",
	  explain: "Gordon v. State holds that a court may not order restitution for losses arising from an offense the defendant was not found criminally responsible for; because the jury acquitted Gordon of causing the prisoner's death, ordering funeral expenses as restitution was a denial of due process. Listed in IRG Ch. 28 (Use of Force) with Garner and Graham; holding is from the case itself, as the IRG states no holding. [Category: Case Law]"
	}
  ];
